# Line endings are normalised to LF everywhere -- in the repository *and* in
# the working tree.
#
# Without this, Git on Windows (`core.autocrlf=true`, the default) stores LF
# but checks out CRLF, so the same file is byte-different depending on who
# cloned it. That is invisible until it isn't: a whole-file diff the first
# time a Linux contributor touches anything, and a class of failure where a
# shell script or Dockerfile with CRLF endings dies with an unhelpful message
# about a command not being found -- the `\r` is part of the argument.
#
# Added at the first commit deliberately. The working tree is already LF, so
# this pins the current state rather than rewriting anything; doing it later,
# after a checkout has introduced CRLF, means a renormalisation commit that
# touches every file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Fatal if they ever get CRLF, so stated explicitly rather than relying on the
# wildcard above surviving future edits to this file.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
docker/**       text eol=lf

# Never touched. `favicon.ico` is the only one in the tree today; the rest are
# here so a future image or font cannot be silently corrupted by newline
# translation. (SVG is deliberately absent -- it is XML, and benefits from
# being diffable text.)
*.ico           binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.webp          binary
*.pdf           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary

# Generated dependency graphs. Still diffable when you need it, but collapsed
# in review by default -- a lockfile churn of several thousand lines otherwise
# buries the change that caused it.
uv.lock                     linguist-generated=true
frontend/package-lock.json  linguist-generated=true
